 When it comes to threads, very little is guaranteed.

 Thread - Java thread is the smallest unit of execution within a program.
          It is a lightweight subprocess that runs independently but shares
          the same memory space as the process, allowing multiple tasks to execute concurrently.


          Thread Creation

                                   Thread
                       ______________|______________
                      |                             |
               extends|                             |implements
                      |                             |
                  Thread                         Runnable
                      |                             |
                      v                             v
           +-----------------+            +----------------------+
           |  Subclass of    |            |   Class implements   |
           |    Thread       |            |      Runnable        |
           +-----------------+            +----------------------+
                      |                             |
                      | override run()              | override run()
                      |                             |
                      | new MyThread()              | new Thread(new MyRunnable())
                      |                             |
                      v                             v
                 myThread.start()           thread.start()



          Thread Life Cycle


                          +----------------+
                          |     New        |
                          +----------------+
                                  |
                          New Thread()
                                  |
                                  v
                          +----------------+
                          |   Runnable     |
                          +----------------+
                                  |
                               start()
                                  |
                                  v
                          +----------------+
                          |    Running     |
                          +----------------+
                                  |
                    --------------------------------
                    |                              |
                run()                         sleep(), wait()
                    |                              |
                    v                              v
            End of execution               +----------------+
                    |                     |    Waiting     |
                    |                     +----------------+
                    |                              |
                    |------------------------------|
                                  |
                                  v
                          +----------------+
                          |     Dead       |
                          +----------------+

 A thread in Java begins as an instance of java.lang.Thread.

 - start()
 - yield()
 - sleep()
 - run()



 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Feature                | WHY (Purpose)                          | WHERE (Use Case)
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Async                 | Non-blocking tasks                      | APIs, UI, file, DB calls
 Atomic                | Lock-free thread-safe variables         | Counters, shared flags
 Concurrent Collections| Thread-safe structures                  | Servers, messaging queues
 Executors             | Manage thread pools                     | Web apps, async tasks
 Locks                 | Advanced thread control                 | Timed locks, fairness
 Parallelism           | Use multi-core CPUs                     | Heavy computations
 Scheduling            | Run tasks automatically                 | Cron jobs, background tasks
 Synchronizers         | Coordinate multiple threads             | Latches, barriers, semaphores
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


✅ 1. Thread (Oldest)

Basic thread creation.

✅ 2. Runnable (Improved Task Representation)

Task separated from thread.

✅ 3. Executors / ExecutorService (Thread Pooling)

Manages a pool of threads.

⭐ AFTER EXECUTORS → NEXT LEVEL CONCURRENCY COMPONENTS
✅ 4. Callable (returns value + throws exception)

Better than Runnable because it can return data.

Callable<Integer> task = () -> 10;

✅ 5. Future (Result of Callable)

Used to get the result.

Future<Integer> f = service.submit(task);

✅ 6. FutureTask (Hybrid: Runnable + Future)

Used to wrap tasks, mostly for advanced frameworks.

⭐ MODERN CONCURRENCY (Java 8+ and Java 21)
✅ 7. CompletableFuture (Asynchronous Programming)

Non-blocking, pipelines, async tasks.

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "Hello");

✅ 8. Locks (ReentrantLock, ReadWriteLock)

Advanced control instead of synchronized.

✅ 9. Atomic Classes (AtomicInteger, AtomicReference)

Lock-free thread-safe operations.

✅ 10. Concurrent Collections

ConcurrentHashMap

CopyOnWriteArrayList

BlockingQueue

ConcurrentLinkedQueue

✅ 11. Synchronizers

Tools for coordinating threads:

CountDownLatch

CyclicBarrier

Semaphore

Phaser

Exchanger

⭐ MODERN ERA (Java 19–21)
✅ 12. Virtual Threads (Project Loom)

Lightweight threads, millions of threads possible.

Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> doWork());